22:25:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really powerful point, and I appreciate you being so honest about it. It’s not weird that it feels strange—it *is* strange to have one voice say something completely different from everyone you trust. That feeling of “common sense” being contradicted is genuinely unsettling, so let’s talk about why that happens and what it actually means. ### The Comfort of Consensus When family, friends, and coworkers all agree on something, it creates a powerful social reality. We’re wired to accept the views of our “in-group” as a mental shortcut—it’s efficient, it signals belonging, and it’s rarely challenged in daily life. If everyone around you nods along, the idea feels true regardless of the evidence. This is called *social proof*, and it’s one of the strongest psychological forces we have. ### When “Common Sense” Is Actually a Local Echo Chamber The problem is that social proof isn’t a truth-detector; it’s a conformity-detector. What feels like “common sense” is often just the prevailing view in a particular time and place. Consider: - A century ago, in many American communities, it was “common sense” that racial segregation was natural and right. - In certain circles, it was once “common sense” that women were intellectually incapable of voting or holding office. - In other parts of the world, it’s “common sense” that the U.S. is a uniquely violent, imperialist nation based on the news they consume. The fact that everyone around you agrees doesn’t mean they’ve independently examined the evidence. More likely, they’re all drawing from the same limited media diet, the same cultural narratives, and the same lack of personal contact with Muslims. That doesn’t make them bad people—it makes them human. But it also means their consensus might be a carefully constructed illusion, not a reflection of reality. ### Why This Specific Idea Feels Like Common Sense The narrative that Islam is uniquely violent has been heavily marketed in certain media